So how do you suppose to get into/out of your place if the door is
locked, you managed to lose the key and there's no back door? The back
doors are there for a reason ...
Beyond a certain level, extra lock security doesn't add much more
protection against unlawful entry, but on the flip side makes it a lot
more difficult to bypass the lock for legitimate reasons.

> Dungeon skeleton keys aren't a "back door" by any stretch - they lie
> all about and any untrained yokel can use them.

Well, yes. The real-world skeleton key is not so much a back door but
rather a sign of the general weakness of the technology.

> Why have locks or keys at all?

For exactly the reason people had locks and keys of NetHack's tech
level: to prevent access by beings unable to operate a lock/key and to
reduce the temptation to people who could; and as a 'do not disturb'
sign.

With this sort of locking technology, locks aren't there to keep out
determined thieves and burglars, but to make access sufficiently
difficult that borderline lawful citizens will think again and that
they consume so much time opening the lock that a guard may notice.

Better lock technology would be practically pointless anyway when
anyone can kick down the doors and break locks of boxes and chests.


And in the end it comes down to gameplay (as Kent pointed out) -
locked containers and doors add a level to gameplay, having a single
key type for all locks gets rid of an inventory management mini game
of little merit.

